SecurityReceipts — IG-2026-004
What Researchers Reported

Researchers tied Zestix/Sentap to data theft from dozens of organizations using credentials obtained from information-stealing malware.

The affected environments included ShareFile, ownCloud, and Nextcloud deployments.

What ownCloud Clarified

ownCloud publicly stated that its software platform had not been compromised through a zero-day or product vulnerability in the reported attacks.

The documented attack path instead involved:

Credentials stolen from employee devices
Valid username/password authentication
Accounts without MFA

What Changed the Understanding

Superficial interpretation: Cloud file-sharing platforms were hacked.

Better-supported interpretation: Attackers used stolen credentials to authenticate into customer-managed environments where authentication controls were insufficient.

That distinction matters because remediation is different.

A software exploit leads defenders toward:

Patch → mitigate vulnerability

This campaign leads defenders toward:

Invalidate credentials → eradicate infostealers → enforce MFA → review session access → audit data access

What Remains Unclear
Whether every victim lacked MFA
Whether some credentials were reused across services
Whether session tokens were also stolen
How old the credentials were
Whether passwords had remained valid for years
Whether organizations detected the access internally
